INT. STRAND FARMHOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A big country kitchen: scarred oak table, iron range, a window onto black fields. A hurricane lamp. A radio on the counter.

HAZEL STRAND, 40s, in a flannel nightgown with a shawl, stands at the sink pumping water. Her brother-in-law EWAN BLYTHE, 50s, leans against the pantry door with a shotgun broken over his arm. At the table, MAGS DELANEY, 12, the neighbor's girl, sits wrapped in a quilt, her feet drawn up under her.

HAZEL
Mags, drink your milk.

MAGS
It's cold.

HAZEL
It's meant to be.

Ewan peers through the window curtains.

EWAN
Nothing yet.

HAZEL
There won't be.

EWAN
You don't know that.

HAZEL
I do.

The radio crackles. A man's voice, thin with static.

DEPUTY CUTTER (V.O.)
(radio)
Strand place, this is Cutter. Come in.

Ewan snaps the shotgun shut and crosses to the radio. Hazel lifts Mags's chin with one finger and checks her eyes. Mags pulls away and tucks the quilt up to her ears.

EWAN
Cutter. Go ahead.

DEPUTY CUTTER (V.O.)
(radio)
Roads are washed out. I can't get to you till morning.

EWAN
Can't or won't?

DEPUTY CUTTER (V.O.)
(radio)
Can't. The bridge is gone.

Hazel sets down the pail. Ewan turns the dial a quarter turn. Static swells.

HAZEL
Ewan, don't.

EWAN
Cutter, you hear anything out there?

DEPUTY CUTTER (V.O.)
(radio, faint)
Like what?

EWAN
Like something in the corn.

Silence on the radio. Then a long, wet scraping sound comes through the speaker. It is not coming from the speaker. It is coming from outside the kitchen window.

MAGS
(whispering)
It's the same sound as at our house.

HAZEL
What sound, sweetheart?

MAGS
When they took my mom.

Ewan raises the shotgun. Hazel snuffs the lamp with a pinch of her fingers. The kitchen goes black. Moonlight picks out the window.

DEPUTY CUTTER (V.O.)
(radio)
Strand place? Strand place, do you copy?

Something taps the glass, once, with a long fingernail.

EWAN
(barely audible)
Hazel, get her under the table.

HAZEL
Already there.

Hazel pulls Mags beneath the oak, and the three of them hold their breath as the tapping travels along the window, around the corner, toward the door.
